WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 11 Student Video
Unit 3, Lesson 3: DNA

1
00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.330
Take a look at the room around you and ask yourself a simple question.

2
00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:09.350
What do you think is the most complex system that stores the most information?

3
00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.930
Maybe there's a computer in the room.

4
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.350
Maybe there's a tablet. Maybe you're streaming this on your cell phone.

5
00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:20.130
Are those the most complex systems that store vast amounts of information?

6
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.650
You know, the answer to that is clearly no.

7
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.190
The most complex system in the room is you.

8
00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.210
In fact, to be specific, it's your DNA.

9
00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:34.730
DNA stores information, much of the information that makes you,

10
00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:40.610
you. In fact, DNA has greater storage capacity than any

11
00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.710
technology humans have ever created.

12
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:47.510
Take for example, just one gram of DNA.

13
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.570
It has roughly the storage capacity of half a million movies.

14
00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.070
Now that blows your smartphone away.

15
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:56.930
Your

16
00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.200
body is made up of around one hundred trillion cells.

17
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:13.020
The DNA in one cell in your body, just one cell if they were unwound, would be about

18
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.040
nine feet in length. That means all the DNA in your body strung together would go

19
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.260
from here to the Sun and back about 70 times.

20
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:28.520
This raises a very troubling and difficult question both for naturalism and for

21
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:34.420
pantheism. What can best explain the source of this vast amount of information?

22
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:35.400
What worldview

23
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.420
best explains the origin of DNA?

24
00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.410
To explain the rise of information that we see in nature, some naturalists have

25
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:53.070
devised a test simply called the monkeys typing Shakespeare theorem.

26
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.770
And the idea is if you have a bunch of monkeys and enough computers and enough time.

27
00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:03.210
Eventually, one of the monkeys will sit down, through a purely blind lucky process,

28
00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.830
and type out all the works of Shakespeare.

29
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:12.730
The idea is to try to explain the rise of information in nature, and in

30
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.370
DNA specifically, through a purely blind process.

31
00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:22.310
In my book Understanding Intelligent Design, my co-author William Dembski and I

32
00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.910
cited a quantum computational physicist by the name of Seth Lloyd.

33
00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:32.790
What Seth Lloyd did is mathematically compute exactly how much information could

34
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:37.310
arise by chance given all the resources of the universe.

35
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.710
What did he conclude? Well, he concluded that chance alone, again with all the

36
00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.490
resources of the universe, could not produce all the works of Shakespeare, couldn't

37
00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.290
produce one work of Shakespeare, not a chapter, not a page, not a paragraph.

38
00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:56.450
All the resources of the universe, by chance, at best could produce four lines

39
00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:02.210
from one work of Shakespeare. To be or not to be, that is the question, whether it

40
00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.430
is nobler in the mind to suffer.

41
00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:09.070
Some time ago, some students and a professor decided to actually

42
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.010
test the monkeys typing Shakespeare theorem.

43
00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:17.050
So they took some monkeys, put them in a zoo with some old computers and typewriters

44
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:21.130
to see if one of the monkeys would sit down and type out Romeo and Juliet or Hamlet.

45
00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:26.410
Well, what happened? First monkey picked up a rock, climbed up on the computer and

46
00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.390
tried to bash it. Some other monkeys climbed up on top and actually went to the

47
00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:35.950
bathroom on the computer. But before you discount this experiment, you know what

48
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:36.230
happened?

49
00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.950
Actually, in the time that they were in there, these monkeys produced roughly six

50
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:46.390
pages of written text. A string of A's, L's, M's,

51
00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:51.410
J's and an occasional S. Not a single word.

52
00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:59.390
In reality, whenever we come across information and can trace it back to its causal

53
00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:05.230
source, we always find a mind. In fact, imagine you come visit me in Southern

54
00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.310
California and we go walking on the beach.

55
00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:12.150
And we walk along. And all of a sudden we stop because we see engraved in the sand a

56
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.150
heart that says, Sean loves Stephanie.

57
00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.490
Stephanie is my wife. Now, I know none of you would stop and go, well, that's crazy.

58
00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:22.230
I've heard about earthquakes in Southern California.

59
00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:26.750
But I had no idea when the ground shook, messages arose from the deep.

60
00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.910
None of you would stop and try to explain that message by tides or the water

61
00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:38.610
or the wind in some fashion. You would know that the only plausible explanation for

62
00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.350
those markings is an intelligent mind.

63
00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:48.770
We know this. In our daily lives, whenever we see information at its source, we

64
00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:52.430
find a mind. For example, you get a text and it's legible.

65
00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:56.290
Trace it back to a texter. Look at the information in a book.

66
00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:01.910
Trace it back to an author. Read a blog and you trace it back to a blogger.

67
00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.830
If you go outside and the clouds say, drink Coke, none of you think, oh my goodness,

68
00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.910
a storm is coming from the east.

69
00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:13.630
Rather, you would know that that information was put there by an intelligent mind,

70
00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:19.270
in this case, by a pilot. We find more information in DNA than in

71
00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:25.190
anything humans can invent. That's why the best explanation for DNA we see in

72
00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:29.170
nature and in the human body in particular is that it comes from an intelligent

73
00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:31.770
designer, an author of life.

